# NoOp — the effect that does nothing, for when doing nothing must be said.

## An effect with no meaning and no consequence.
##
## It exists so that "this computation performs nothing" can be STATED rather
## than inferred from silence. A slot declared `<*>` — `comptime`'s `eval` is
## the one in the language today — asks for a body that performs its effects and
## handles them itself. A body that genuinely needs no effects still has to
## answer that, and this is how:
##
##     comptime { eval = {} -> perform NoOpHandler in do
##         let _ = NoOp.noop {}
##         factorial { n = 5 }
##     }
##
## The alternative would be to let a slot meaning "effects were handled here"
## silently accept a body where none were, which erases exactly the distinction
## `<*>` exists to draw: a pure body and a fully-discharged one leave the SAME
## empty residual row, so the residual cannot tell them apart. Performing `NoOp`
## is the author saying which of the two they meant.
effect NoOp where {
  ## Do nothing, and be seen doing it.
  noop: {} -> {} <NoOp>
}

## The only handler `NoOp` needs — it does nothing, which is the whole point.
impl NoOp for NoOpHandler where {
  noop = {} -> resume { val = {} }
}

## Perform-and-handle `NoOp` in one call, so a computation that needs no effects
## can still say so without spelling out a handler:
##
##     comptime { eval = {} -> do
##         noop {}
##         factorial { n = 5 }
##     }
##
## `performed` propagates through the call, so this satisfies a `<*>` slot
## exactly as an inline `perform` would.
pub let noop: {} -> {} <*> = {} -> perform NoOpHandler in NoOp.noop {}
